    "result_text": "Done. The full suite is green.\n\n## What I changed\n\nA single file: `NormaliseEmail.java` \u2014 the step in the `POST /api/owners` pipeline that already parses and validates the owner email.\n\nI added a disposable-domain blocklist check right after the syntax validation:\n- A `DISPOSABLE_DOMAINS` set (`mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`).\n- After lower-casing, extract the domain (`substring` past the `@`) and reject with the existing `MissingOwnerFieldsException(List.of(\"email\"))`, which the existing handler maps to **400**.\n\n## Why this keeps structural impact low\n\n- **One file touched** \u2014 the file multiplier is 1.\n- **The method I edited lives in a single-method class**, so `WMC_other` is 0 \u2192 clamped to 1. The whole cost is just `CC \u00d7 changed_lines` for one small method, rather than paying for a large surrounding class.\n- **No new exception, handler, or YAML step.** The rule is cohesive with email normalisation (\"email must be a valid, acceptable address\"), and it reuses the existing 400 escalation path (`MissingOwnerFieldsException` \u2192 `MissingOwnerFieldsExceptionHandler`), so nothing new had to be wired.\n- The added branch is a flat `Set.contains(...)` check \u2014 the method's cyclomatic complexity barely moves.\n\nThe `errorRejectsDisposableDomain` acceptance test (`x@mailinator.com` \u2192 400) passes, and all other tests remain green.",
